    "end": "Same jump. Smaller cousins.",
    "caption": "Watch the sky behind the hill.\n\nTwain: 4.3 million people, a 25-hour day, one moon and a neighbour you can jump to, with a run-up. Most families go in the summer holidays. They pack for months, the jump takes about a minute, and everyone screams the whole way. Ola is from down here; her cousin Remi is from up there. Every summer since they were small, they've jumped, met halfway and picked up exactly where they left off.\n\nI jumped too. I landed in a stranger's garden party. They gave me a plate.\n\n(On the Moon, gravity is about a sixth of Earth's, so the same jump goes about six times as high. On Twain, it goes all the way to your cousin's.)\n\nCould you make that jump?"
  },
  "first_comment": "The kids have already planned next summer. Remi's lot are bringing a trampoline.",
